1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,564
Sweetheart, in the winter of nineteen seventy-two,

2
00:00:03,564 --> 00:00:05,489
I was a waitress at the Elm

3
00:00:05,489 --> 00:00:08,056
Street Diner, and a skinny boy about

4
00:00:08,056 --> 00:00:10,266
twelve years old came in out of

5
00:00:10,266 --> 00:00:12,833
the snow every night at closing time

6
00:00:12,833 --> 00:00:15,257
and stole the dinner rolls off the

7
00:00:15,257 --> 00:00:16,184
dirty plates.

8
00:00:16,364 --> 00:00:18,779
My boss wanted to call the police.

9
00:00:18,959 --> 00:00:22,073
Instead, I did something that got me

10
00:00:22,073 --> 00:00:22,593
fired.

11
00:00:22,773 --> 00:00:24,437
And thirty years later,

12
00:00:24,437 --> 00:00:27,115
that boy walked back through my door.

13
00:00:27,464 --> 00:00:30,362
Tonight I'm going to tell you exactly

14
00:00:30,362 --> 00:00:32,241
what I did for that boy,

15
00:00:32,241 --> 00:00:33,963
why it cost me my job,

16
00:00:33,963 --> 00:00:36,312
and what he was holding in his

17
00:00:36,312 --> 00:00:38,113
hand when he came back,

18
00:00:38,113 --> 00:00:39,601
thirty years later,

19
00:00:39,601 --> 00:00:40,932
on Christmas Eve.

20
00:00:41,112 --> 00:00:42,034
Stay with me,

21
00:00:42,034 --> 00:00:44,728
honey, because I've told this story at

22
00:00:44,728 --> 00:00:46,785
every Christmas dinner since,

23
00:00:46,785 --> 00:00:48,912
and the grown men in my family

24
00:00:48,912 --> 00:00:51,110
still have to leave the room at

25
00:00:51,110 --> 00:00:51,677
the end.

26
00:00:52,027 --> 00:00:54,307
The Elm Street Diner was a long

27
00:00:54,307 --> 00:00:57,028
silver building with red stools and a

28
00:00:57,028 --> 00:00:59,748
jukebox that only played Patsy Cline.

29
00:00:59,928 --> 00:01:01,780
I worked the night shift,

30
00:01:01,780 --> 00:01:02,892
four to eleven,

31
00:01:02,892 --> 00:01:05,189
for a dollar sixty an hour plus

32
00:01:05,189 --> 00:01:05,559
tips.

33
00:01:05,739 --> 00:01:09,023
My husband Harold was working double shifts

34
00:01:09,023 --> 00:01:10,016
at the plant,

35
00:01:10,016 --> 00:01:12,230
and we had two little ones at

36
00:01:12,230 --> 00:01:12,612
home.

37
00:01:12,792 --> 00:01:14,301
Every penny mattered.

38
00:01:14,481 --> 00:01:15,735
That job mattered.

39
00:01:16,085 --> 00:01:18,370
My boss was a man named Earl

40
00:01:18,370 --> 00:01:18,942
Pruitt.

41
00:01:19,122 --> 00:01:21,505
Earl wasn't a bad man,

42
00:01:21,505 --> 00:01:22,372
exactly.

43
00:01:22,552 --> 00:01:24,141
He was a tired man,

44
00:01:24,141 --> 00:01:25,478
and a tight one.

45
00:01:25,658 --> 00:01:27,701
He counted the sugar packets.

46
00:01:27,881 --> 00:01:30,241
He made us scrape the butter pats

47
00:01:30,241 --> 00:01:32,386
off the plates and reuse them.

48
00:01:32,566 --> 00:01:33,878
And he had a rule,

49
00:01:33,878 --> 00:01:36,722
posted right over the coffee machine in

50
00:01:36,722 --> 00:01:37,524
red marker.

51
00:01:37,704 --> 00:01:39,085
No loitering.

52
00:01:39,265 --> 00:01:40,206
No credit.

53
00:01:40,386 --> 00:01:41,941
No free food.

54
00:01:42,121 --> 00:01:42,946
Ever.

55
00:01:43,296 --> 00:01:45,547
The first time I saw the boy,

56
00:01:45,547 --> 00:01:48,265
it was the second week of December.

57
00:01:48,445 --> 00:01:50,763
I was bussing a table near the

58
00:01:50,763 --> 00:01:53,159
back door, and I turned around,

59
00:01:53,159 --> 00:01:54,473
and there he was,

60
00:01:54,473 --> 00:01:56,637
a skinny kid in a jacket two

61
00:01:56,637 --> 00:01:57,873
sizes too small,

62
00:01:57,873 --> 00:02:00,965
stuffing a half-eaten dinner roll in his

63
00:02:00,965 --> 00:02:01,506
pocket.

64
00:02:01,686 --> 00:02:03,172
He looked at me.

65
00:02:03,352 --> 00:02:04,559
I looked at him.

66
00:02:04,739 --> 00:02:06,743
And then he ran out that back

67
00:02:06,743 --> 00:02:09,023
door into the snow like a rabbit.

68
00:02:09,374 --> 00:02:11,626
He came back the next night.

69
00:02:11,806 --> 00:02:12,932
And the next.

70
00:02:13,112 --> 00:02:14,670
Always at ten thirty,

71
00:02:14,670 --> 00:02:15,932
right at closing,

72
00:02:15,932 --> 00:02:18,232
when the plates were piling up.

73
00:02:18,412 --> 00:02:20,490
He'd slip in the back,

74
00:02:20,490 --> 00:02:22,662
grab whatever was left,

75
00:02:22,662 --> 00:02:23,985
and disappear.

76
00:02:24,165 --> 00:02:26,612
I started leaving the rolls a little

77
00:02:26,612 --> 00:02:27,903
closer to the door.

78
00:02:28,083 --> 00:02:29,801
Then a piece of chicken.

79
00:02:29,981 --> 00:02:32,409
Then half a hamburger that a trucker

80
00:02:32,409 --> 00:02:33,488
hadn't finished.

81
00:02:33,668 --> 00:02:35,436
I never said a word,

82
00:02:35,436 --> 00:02:37,116
and neither did he.

83
00:02:37,466 --> 00:02:39,805
One night, about a week in,

84
00:02:39,805 --> 00:02:41,018
he didn't run.

85
00:02:41,198 --> 00:02:43,784
He stood there by the back door,

86
00:02:43,784 --> 00:02:45,885
holding a cold french fry,

87
00:02:45,885 --> 00:02:46,855
and he said,

88
00:02:46,855 --> 00:02:48,794
very quietly, thank you,

89
00:02:48,794 --> 00:02:49,279
ma'am.

90
00:02:49,459 --> 00:02:50,439
And I said,

91
00:02:50,439 --> 00:02:51,954
what's your name,

92
00:02:51,954 --> 00:02:52,489
honey?

93
00:02:52,669 --> 00:02:54,352
He said, Danny.

94
00:02:54,532 --> 00:02:56,436
Same as my little brother.

95
00:02:56,616 --> 00:02:59,101
I asked where his mother was.

96
00:02:59,281 --> 00:03:01,458
He looked at the floor and said,

97
00:03:01,458 --> 00:03:02,207
she's sick.

98
00:03:02,387 --> 00:03:04,826
And I knew exactly what kind of

99
00:03:04,826 --> 00:03:05,928
sick he meant.

100
00:03:06,278 --> 00:03:08,481
Danny lived with his mother in a

101
00:03:08,481 --> 00:03:10,753
room above the pool hall on Third

102
00:03:10,753 --> 00:03:11,235
Street.

103
00:03:11,415 --> 00:03:12,355
No heat.

104
00:03:12,535 --> 00:03:14,846
No food in the icebox.

105
00:03:15,026 --> 00:03:17,782
She drank whatever money came into that

106
00:03:17,782 --> 00:03:20,608
room, and Danny had been feeding himself

107
00:03:20,608 --> 00:03:23,222
out of garbage cans since the summer.

108
00:03:23,402 --> 00:03:25,241
He was twelve years old,

109
00:03:25,241 --> 00:03:25,701
honey.

110
00:03:25,881 --> 00:03:26,729
Twelve.

111
00:03:26,909 --> 00:03:29,368
In a minute I'll tell you what

112
00:03:29,368 --> 00:03:30,680
he asked me for,

113
00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:32,238
and it wasn't food.

114
00:03:32,588 --> 00:03:36,256
After that, I stopped leaving him leftovers.

115
00:03:36,436 --> 00:03:38,956
I started making him a real plate.

116
00:03:39,136 --> 00:03:40,885
A hamburger, fries,

117
00:03:40,885 --> 00:03:42,358
a glass of milk,

118
00:03:42,358 --> 00:03:44,108
and a piece of pie,

119
00:03:44,108 --> 00:03:47,147
every single night at ten thirty.

120
00:03:47,327 --> 00:03:49,236
I paid for it out of my

121
00:03:49,236 --> 00:03:50,647
tips, sweetheart.

122
00:03:50,827 --> 00:03:53,555
Every penny, written down on a ticket

123
00:03:53,555 --> 00:03:55,398
and paid at the register,

124
00:03:55,398 --> 00:03:57,758
so nobody could ever say I stole

125
00:03:57,758 --> 00:03:57,979
it.

126
00:03:58,159 --> 00:04:00,197
It came to about a dollar ten

127
00:04:00,197 --> 00:04:00,759
a night.

128
00:04:01,110 --> 00:04:04,056
If you're enjoying Grandma's story,

129
00:04:04,056 --> 00:04:06,328
tap the like button for me,

130
00:04:06,328 --> 00:04:06,833
honey.

131
00:04:07,013 --> 00:04:10,264
It helps more people find these stories.

132
00:04:10,444 --> 00:04:13,022
Danny would sit on an upside-down milk

133
00:04:13,022 --> 00:04:15,261
crate in the back hallway and eat

134
00:04:15,261 --> 00:04:16,075
every crumb.

135
00:04:16,255 --> 00:04:18,953
And then he'd do his homework there,

136
00:04:18,953 --> 00:04:21,202
at the little shelf by the mop

137
00:04:21,202 --> 00:04:23,000
sink, while I closed up.

138
00:04:23,180 --> 00:04:24,249
He was smart,

139
00:04:24,249 --> 00:04:25,154
sweetheart.

140
00:04:25,334 --> 00:04:26,832
Sharp as a tack.

141
00:04:27,012 --> 00:04:29,531
He wanted to know how everything worked.

142
00:04:29,881 --> 00:04:32,150
One night he asked me the thing

143
00:04:32,150 --> 00:04:33,247
I promised you.

144
00:04:33,427 --> 00:04:35,336
He didn't ask for money,

145
00:04:35,336 --> 00:04:36,131
or a coat,

146
00:04:36,131 --> 00:04:37,642
or anything to eat.

147
00:04:37,822 --> 00:04:39,743
He asked me if I had any

148
00:04:39,743 --> 00:04:40,864
books at home.

149
00:04:41,044 --> 00:04:42,472
Any books at all.

150
00:04:42,652 --> 00:04:44,644
He said the library in town made

151
00:04:44,644 --> 00:04:46,636
you have a parent sign the card,

152
00:04:46,636 --> 00:04:48,753
and his mother couldn't get out of

153
00:04:48,753 --> 00:04:49,002
bed.

154
00:04:49,182 --> 00:04:51,876
So I brought him my Harold's old

155
00:04:51,876 --> 00:04:54,906
encyclopedias, one volume at a time.

156
00:04:55,256 --> 00:04:57,605
On the twenty-third of December,

157
00:04:57,605 --> 00:05:00,469
Earl came back early from his brother's

158
00:05:00,469 --> 00:05:00,910
house.

159
00:05:01,090 --> 00:05:03,196
He walked in the back door at

160
00:05:03,196 --> 00:05:06,173
ten forty and found a twelve-year-old boy

161
00:05:06,173 --> 00:05:07,335
on a milk crate,

162
00:05:07,335 --> 00:05:09,805
eating pie and reading volume M of

163
00:05:09,805 --> 00:05:11,039
the encyclopedia.

164
00:05:11,219 --> 00:05:13,367
Earl's face turned purple.

165
00:05:13,547 --> 00:05:15,869
He said, what is this?

166
00:05:16,049 --> 00:05:17,152
I told you.

167
00:05:17,332 --> 00:05:20,486
No loitering, no free food,

168
00:05:20,486 --> 00:05:21,071
ever.

169
00:05:21,251 --> 00:05:23,398
Get that kid out of here.

170
00:05:23,749 --> 00:05:25,618
I showed him the tickets.

171
00:05:25,798 --> 00:05:27,194
Every single one,

172
00:05:27,194 --> 00:05:29,083
paid with my own money.

173
00:05:29,263 --> 00:05:31,086
Earl didn't care.

174
00:05:31,266 --> 00:05:33,913
He said, I don't run a charity.

175
00:05:34,093 --> 00:05:36,355
That kid's mother is a drunk,

176
00:05:36,355 --> 00:05:37,681
and he's a thief,

177
00:05:37,681 --> 00:05:40,177
and he'll be in jail by sixteen.

178
00:05:40,357 --> 00:05:42,279
And if you feed him again,

179
00:05:42,279 --> 00:05:43,167
you're done.

180
00:05:43,347 --> 00:05:45,583
Danny was standing right there,

181
00:05:45,583 --> 00:05:46,377
sweetheart.

182
00:05:46,557 --> 00:05:48,298
He heard every word.

183
00:05:48,478 --> 00:05:51,125
I saw his face close up like

184
00:05:51,125 --> 00:05:51,787
a fist.

185
00:05:52,137 --> 00:05:54,087
I took off my apron.

186
00:05:54,267 --> 00:05:56,919
I folded it up nice and neat,

187
00:05:56,919 --> 00:05:59,571
and I laid it on the counter,

188
00:05:59,571 --> 00:06:00,577
and I said,

189
00:06:00,577 --> 00:06:02,406
Earl, then I'm done.

190
00:06:02,586 --> 00:06:04,835
And I took Danny by the hand,

191
00:06:04,835 --> 00:06:07,162
and I walked him out that back

192
00:06:07,162 --> 00:06:08,636
door into the snow,

193
00:06:08,636 --> 00:06:10,653
two days before Christmas,

194
00:06:10,653 --> 00:06:12,902
with no job and two babies at

195
00:06:12,902 --> 00:06:13,290
home.

196
00:06:13,470 --> 00:06:15,537
Harold was going to kill me.

197
00:06:15,717 --> 00:06:18,039
I knew it the whole walk home.

198
00:06:18,389 --> 00:06:20,350
Harold didn't kill me,

199
00:06:20,350 --> 00:06:20,885
honey.

200
00:06:21,065 --> 00:06:22,339
When I told him,

201
00:06:22,339 --> 00:06:24,410
he sat quiet for a minute,

202
00:06:24,410 --> 00:06:25,764
and then he said,

203
00:06:25,764 --> 00:06:29,029
Ruthie, where's the boy sleeping tonight?

204
00:06:29,209 --> 00:06:31,442
And that's how Danny came to spend

205
00:06:31,442 --> 00:06:34,463
Christmas of nineteen seventy-two on the couch

206
00:06:34,463 --> 00:06:35,711
in our living room.

207
00:06:35,891 --> 00:06:38,140
My mother came over and cut his

208
00:06:38,140 --> 00:06:38,503
hair.

209
00:06:38,683 --> 00:06:41,400
Harold gave him his old winter coat.

210
00:06:41,580 --> 00:06:43,310
And on Christmas morning,

211
00:06:43,310 --> 00:06:45,664
there was a present under our tree

212
00:06:45,664 --> 00:06:47,048
with his name on it.

213
00:06:47,398 --> 00:06:49,518
It was a library card,

214
00:06:49,518 --> 00:06:50,579
sweetheart.

215
00:06:50,759 --> 00:06:53,247
Harold had gone down to the library

216
00:06:53,247 --> 00:06:56,020
on Christmas Eve and signed it himself,

217
00:06:56,020 --> 00:06:57,158
as his guardian,

218
00:06:57,158 --> 00:06:59,433
and he'd wrapped it in the funny

219
00:06:59,433 --> 00:06:59,931
papers.

220
00:07:00,111 --> 00:07:02,739
Danny opened that box and looked at

221
00:07:02,739 --> 00:07:04,015
that little card,

222
00:07:04,015 --> 00:07:06,418
and he cried so hard he couldn't

223
00:07:06,418 --> 00:07:07,019
breathe.

224
00:07:07,199 --> 00:07:08,847
A library card.

225
00:07:09,027 --> 00:07:11,303
Of all the presents I've ever seen

226
00:07:11,303 --> 00:07:13,578
opened, that's the one I remember.

227
00:07:13,929 --> 00:07:15,416
After New Year's,

228
00:07:15,416 --> 00:07:17,342
the county stepped in.

229
00:07:17,522 --> 00:07:20,110
Danny's mother went to a hospital in

230
00:07:20,110 --> 00:07:22,769
Columbus, and Danny went to live with

231
00:07:22,769 --> 00:07:24,279
an aunt in Cleveland.

232
00:07:24,459 --> 00:07:26,968
We put him on a Greyhound bus

233
00:07:26,968 --> 00:07:28,525
with a sack lunch,

234
00:07:28,525 --> 00:07:31,726
the encyclopedias in a cardboard box,

235
00:07:31,726 --> 00:07:34,234
and our address on a piece of

236
00:07:34,234 --> 00:07:35,964
paper in his pocket.

237
00:07:36,144 --> 00:07:38,302
He waved out the back window until

238
00:07:38,302 --> 00:07:39,953
the bus turned the corner.

239
00:07:40,133 --> 00:07:42,292
And then I didn't hear from him

240
00:07:42,292 --> 00:07:43,476
for thirty years.

241
00:07:43,826 --> 00:07:45,590
So far, the rolls,

242
00:07:45,590 --> 00:07:47,746
the plates, the books,

243
00:07:47,746 --> 00:07:48,922
and the bus.

244
00:07:49,102 --> 00:07:50,675
Life went on,

245
00:07:50,675 --> 00:07:51,401
honey.

246
00:07:51,581 --> 00:07:54,460
I found another job at the five-and-dime.

247
00:07:54,640 --> 00:07:56,277
Our children grew up.

248
00:07:56,457 --> 00:07:57,990
Harold retired.

249
00:07:58,170 --> 00:07:59,651
And every Christmas,

250
00:07:59,651 --> 00:08:02,094
I'd wonder about that skinny boy.

251
00:08:02,274 --> 00:08:03,400
Did he make it?

252
00:08:03,580 --> 00:08:05,159
Was Earl right?

253
00:08:05,339 --> 00:08:07,545
Was he in jail somewhere?

254
00:08:07,725 --> 00:08:10,382
I'd pray for him at the Christmas

255
00:08:10,382 --> 00:08:12,879
Eve service, every single year,

256
00:08:12,879 --> 00:08:15,214
and I never told anybody why.

257
00:08:15,564 --> 00:08:16,943
On Christmas Eve,

258
00:08:16,943 --> 00:08:18,322
two thousand two,

259
00:08:18,322 --> 00:08:20,674
there was a knock on our door

260
00:08:20,674 --> 00:08:21,891
at six o'clock.

261
00:08:22,071 --> 00:08:23,421
Harold opened it,

262
00:08:23,421 --> 00:08:25,725
and there stood a tall man in

263
00:08:25,725 --> 00:08:26,757
a camel coat,

264
00:08:26,757 --> 00:08:29,855
maybe forty, with a little girl holding

265
00:08:29,855 --> 00:08:30,570
his hand.

266
00:08:30,750 --> 00:08:33,243
He said, excuse me,

267
00:08:33,243 --> 00:08:33,768
sir.

268
00:08:33,948 --> 00:08:36,468
Does Ruth Miller still live here?

269
00:08:36,648 --> 00:08:39,039
Harold said, who's asking?

270
00:08:39,219 --> 00:08:40,579
And the man said,

271
00:08:40,579 --> 00:08:42,180
tell her it's Danny.

272
00:08:42,360 --> 00:08:44,960
Tell her I brought back volume M.

273
00:08:45,311 --> 00:08:46,838
I came to the door,

274
00:08:46,838 --> 00:08:49,090
honey, and I knew him in one

275
00:08:49,090 --> 00:08:49,653
second.

276
00:08:49,833 --> 00:08:51,238
The same eyes.

277
00:08:51,418 --> 00:08:54,652
He was holding that old encyclopedia under

278
00:08:54,652 --> 00:08:56,039
his arm, volume M,

279
00:08:56,039 --> 00:08:59,274
with my Harold's name still written inside

280
00:08:59,274 --> 00:09:00,044
the cover.

281
00:09:00,224 --> 00:09:02,543
He said, I've carried this with me

282
00:09:02,543 --> 00:09:03,976
through two colleges,

283
00:09:03,976 --> 00:09:06,296
four apartments, and three states.

284
00:09:06,476 --> 00:09:08,762
I figured it was time to return

285
00:09:08,762 --> 00:09:08,984
it.

286
00:09:09,164 --> 00:09:11,830
And then that big grown man hugged

287
00:09:11,830 --> 00:09:13,633
me and wouldn't let go.

288
00:09:13,984 --> 00:09:15,354
Danny was Dr.

289
00:09:15,534 --> 00:09:17,008
Daniel Hayes now.

290
00:09:17,188 --> 00:09:19,986
A pediatrician, sweetheart.

291
00:09:20,166 --> 00:09:22,723
A children's doctor in Cleveland,

292
00:09:22,723 --> 00:09:24,427
with his own practice.

293
00:09:24,607 --> 00:09:26,896
The little girl was his daughter,

294
00:09:26,896 --> 00:09:27,382
Ruthie.

295
00:09:27,562 --> 00:09:29,466
He'd named her after me.

296
00:09:29,646 --> 00:09:31,934
He said, I always told her that

297
00:09:31,934 --> 00:09:34,666
a waitress in Mansfield saved my life

298
00:09:34,666 --> 00:09:37,324
with a hamburger and a library card,

299
00:09:37,324 --> 00:09:39,317
and she wanted to meet you.

300
00:09:39,497 --> 00:09:42,060
I sat down right on the stairs,

301
00:09:42,060 --> 00:09:44,211
because my knees gave out.

302
00:09:44,561 --> 00:09:46,964
And then he handed me an envelope.

303
00:09:47,144 --> 00:09:50,270
Inside was a newspaper clipping from the

304
00:09:50,270 --> 00:09:51,521
Cleveland paper.

305
00:09:51,701 --> 00:09:53,373
It said that Dr.

306
00:09:53,553 --> 00:09:56,645
Hayes's clinic had started a program called

307
00:09:56,645 --> 00:09:59,161
Ruth's Table, a free hot meal every

308
00:09:59,161 --> 00:10:01,534
night at the clinic for any child

309
00:10:01,534 --> 00:10:04,338
in the neighborhood who came in hungry.

310
00:10:04,518 --> 00:10:06,074
No questions.

311
00:10:06,254 --> 00:10:07,462
No forms.

312
00:10:07,642 --> 00:10:10,254
No parent signature required.

313
00:10:10,434 --> 00:10:13,347
Eleven thousand meals that year,

314
00:10:13,347 --> 00:10:13,894
honey.

315
00:10:14,074 --> 00:10:15,432
Eleven thousand.

316
00:10:15,782 --> 00:10:17,684
And there's one more thing,

317
00:10:17,684 --> 00:10:19,799
the part people never believe.

318
00:10:19,979 --> 00:10:21,513
Danny asked me,

319
00:10:21,513 --> 00:10:24,274
is Earl Pruitt still alive?

320
00:10:24,454 --> 00:10:25,569
He was.

321
00:10:25,749 --> 00:10:27,487
Eighty-one years old,

322
00:10:27,487 --> 00:10:29,886
in a nursing home on the east

323
00:10:29,886 --> 00:10:30,300
side.

324
00:10:30,480 --> 00:10:32,602
So the day after Christmas,

325
00:10:32,602 --> 00:10:34,410
Danny drove over there,

326
00:10:34,410 --> 00:10:35,275
by himself.

327
00:10:35,455 --> 00:10:38,148
He never told me everything they said.

328
00:10:38,328 --> 00:10:40,763
But Earl's daughter told me later that

329
00:10:40,763 --> 00:10:42,941
her father cried for an hour after

330
00:10:42,941 --> 00:10:43,646
that visit.

331
00:10:43,996 --> 00:10:46,422
Danny told Earl who he was.

332
00:10:46,602 --> 00:10:48,645
The thief on the milk crate.

333
00:10:48,825 --> 00:10:51,190
The boy who'd be in jail by

334
00:10:51,190 --> 00:10:51,891
sixteen.

335
00:10:52,071 --> 00:10:54,304
And then he told Earl that he'd

336
00:10:54,304 --> 00:10:57,474
examined over forty thousand children in his

337
00:10:57,474 --> 00:11:00,068
career, and every single time he saw

338
00:11:00,068 --> 00:11:01,005
a hungry one,

339
00:11:01,005 --> 00:11:03,454
he thought about that night in the

340
00:11:03,454 --> 00:11:05,832
diner, and he decided to be Ruth,

341
00:11:05,832 --> 00:11:06,769
and not Earl.

342
00:11:06,949 --> 00:11:08,646
Then he shook his hand,

343
00:11:08,646 --> 00:11:11,302
and he wished him a merry Christmas.

344
00:11:11,653 --> 00:11:14,855
Danny and his family come every Christmas

345
00:11:14,855 --> 00:11:16,027
Eve now, honey,

346
00:11:16,027 --> 00:11:17,980
even since Harold passed.

347
00:11:18,160 --> 00:11:20,726
Ruthie is in medical school herself.

348
00:11:20,906 --> 00:11:22,178
And every year,

349
00:11:22,178 --> 00:11:25,656
Danny brings a new encyclopedia volume to

350
00:11:25,656 --> 00:11:27,013
put on my shelf.

351
00:11:27,193 --> 00:11:29,120
He's up to volume S.

352
00:11:29,300 --> 00:11:31,516
He says when he gets to Z,

353
00:11:31,516 --> 00:11:33,561
he'll have paid me back.

354
00:11:33,741 --> 00:11:35,620
I tell him he paid me back

355
00:11:35,620 --> 00:11:38,149
the day he walked through that door

356
00:11:38,149 --> 00:11:38,582
alive.

357
00:11:38,932 --> 00:11:41,467
Here's what I want you to remember,

358
00:11:41,467 --> 00:11:42,264
sweetheart.

359
00:11:42,444 --> 00:11:44,815
Earl looked at that boy and saw

360
00:11:44,815 --> 00:11:45,427
a thief.

361
00:11:45,607 --> 00:11:47,456
I looked at him and saw a

362
00:11:47,456 --> 00:11:48,417
hungry child.

363
00:11:48,597 --> 00:11:50,269
It's the same boy.

364
00:11:50,449 --> 00:11:52,598
You get to decide which one you

365
00:11:52,598 --> 00:11:52,875
see.

366
00:11:53,055 --> 00:11:55,061
And whichever one you see,

367
00:11:55,061 --> 00:11:57,839
that's the one you help them become.

368
00:11:58,019 --> 00:11:59,879
A dollar ten a night,

369
00:11:59,879 --> 00:12:00,410
honey.

370
00:12:00,590 --> 00:12:02,622
That's all it cost me.

371
00:12:02,802 --> 00:12:04,695
Best money I ever spent.

372
00:12:05,045 --> 00:12:07,162
Tell me in the comments,

373
00:12:07,162 --> 00:12:07,692
honey.

374
00:12:07,872 --> 00:12:09,729
Would you have quit that job?

375
00:12:09,909 --> 00:12:11,964
Be honest with Grandma.

376
00:12:12,144 --> 00:12:14,789
And if a stranger ever changed your

377
00:12:14,789 --> 00:12:17,055
life, I want to hear about it.

378
00:12:17,235 --> 00:12:19,686
There are more of my family's stories

379
00:12:19,686 --> 00:12:22,005
in the playlist right there on your

380
00:12:22,005 --> 00:12:24,058
screen, and I'll see you in the

381
00:12:24,058 --> 00:12:24,654
next one.

382
00:12:24,834 --> 00:12:26,785
Good night, sweetheart.

383
00:12:26,965 --> 00:12:28,625
Merry Christmas.
